# Copyrighted LeapFrog content — NEVER commit.
# Cartridge/app backups made with LFManager, and anything extracted from them.
games/
rootfs/
sources/
runtime/sysroot/
# The Didj controller overlay, staged by tools/install-didj.sh and reapplied to
# every Didj game it installs. Downloaded, not ours to commit.
runtime/didj/
runtime/libs/
runtime/shimlibs/
runtime/shimlibs-z/
# shimlibs-egl carries libEGLreal.so, which IS LeapFrog's own libEGL with one
# string patched. Build product and their code both; never commit it.
runtime/shimlibs-egl/
reference/device-capture/
reference/

# Build products
tadpole/viewer/tadpole-view
runtime/tone
# The fake net.connman, an ARM binary built from tadpole/shim/tadpole_connman.c.
runtime/tadpole-connman
runtime/tadpole-avahi
# what tools/micromods.py wrote into this checkout's Downloads, so it can
# take exactly that back out again. Local state about a local sysroot.
runtime/micromods-made.json
# Micromod packages downloaded from LeapFrog. Kept so a re-scan or a sysroot
# reset costs the server nothing; it is their content, not ours to redistribute.
runtime/micromods-cache/
*.o

# Captures and scratch
*.raw
*.core
shots/*.wav

# erase-firmware.sh moves the old install here rather than deleting it.
# Hundreds of megabytes of recovered firmware — never commit it.
.erased-*/
tools/glconform/*.log

# EVERYTHING UNDER build/ IS A BUILD PRODUCT, and since the AppImage started
# carrying its own qemu and Python, a large one: 22 MB for the image and 67 MB
# of staged dependencies, per build. Releases carry the binary; git carries the
# source that makes it.
#
# It is also the honest place to draw the line for licensing. The AppImage
# redistributes qemu (GPLv2) and ubi_reader (GPLv3), so shipping it means
# shipping their source too — a release attaching one image, next to
# build/deps/manifest.txt naming the exact upstream builds, is a far easier
# obligation to meet than a repository quietly accumulating vendored binaries.
build/

# The release script. It lives in the working copy rather than in the
# repository: it publishes to one particular GitHub repository, pushes tags, and
# is the maintainer's tool rather than part of what Tadpole is.
post-commit.sh

# Byte-compiled Python from running the tools in place.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Keys live on your machine, never in the repository or an AppImage.
# BOTH SPELLINGS. A git worktree reaches the real folder through a SYMLINK
# named keys, and a pattern ending in / does not match a symlink — so "keys/"
# alone left the key showing up as untracked and one `git add -A` from being
# committed.
keys/
keys
runtime/shimlibs-pkg/
tadpole/.tadpole-version
